Drives its own private GC state (its own `gcBindMemory`/`gcInit`-bound arena),
scripting an alloc/free churn sequence against that private collector, not the
ambient runtime one. Its assertions are on that private collector's own index
behavior (spare-table reuse under sustained tombstone churn) — correct
regardless of whether the ambient (libbitrt) collector ever runs.

CORRECTION: "the ambient collector has nothing live to find and legitimately
never fires under BIT_GC=stress" was false. Measured, aarch64-macos, 5 runs
each policy: default `collections=0` every time; `BIT_GC=stress` 201156,
201091, 201114, 201118, 201210 (denominator: 5/5, spread ~0.06%) — this
program's churn workload is large enough that the count is dominated by its
OWN loop back-edges (ordinary, non-`@nosplit` code), not by stress.bit's
documented ~101 scheduler-loop floor, which is a small fraction of this total.

Every allocation this program makes goes through the private `g` handle, so
the ambient collector never traces or sweeps any of its objects. But this
program's own backing arena (`arena`/`indexArena`, ordinary managed `[]i64`
slices) is used only once, at the `gcBindMemory` call, and never referenced
again in the source — unlike gcbit's `alive` line or gcspanblocks'/
gcspanfree's/gcspanfreelist's `rooted` field, nothing here re-reads the
arena to prove it survived the ~201k ambient collections this run measured.
#4184 tracks whether that is a real gap (the compiler's live-interval-based
stack maps protect a slice only through its LAST textual use) or closed by
some other guarantee; this program's own passing `.expected` comparison is
evidence it does not currently manifest here, not proof the mechanism is
sound.
